# Pinned build tooling for ontology graphs. Ontology versions live in CAC_ONTOLOGY_VERSION;
# this file covers the library that writes graphs, not the vocabularies it writes.

case-uco-sdk:
  repo: https://github.com/vulnmaster/CASE-UCO-SDK
  tag: v1.34.0   (annotated tag object f582e098bcb105f2ac13cb3bcdf93f30d43c72d6)
  commit: 3455cc10682434fbff4ff95e6b9e887700287632   (what the tag peels to; verified with git ls-remote, 2026-11-16)
  package: case-uco 1.34.0 from the python/ subdirectory; depends on rdflib and cdo-local-uuid (pinned 0.5.0)
  install: requirements-sdk.txt (offline research build; not in requirements.txt, not on Railway)
  used by: state_machines/graphs/emit.py (CASEGraph, canonical_hex_binary) for every PACER evidence and
           trajectory graph: court-record structure (investigation, actions, roles, relationships,
           cited annotations) that the SDK's CASE builders produce in canonical form.
           The press-release corpus (ontology/pipeline/features_to_cac.py) is CaseLinker's own mapping
           from its extracted features to CAC terms, serialized with rdflib; the SDK would only
           serialize the same mapping. Both are validated against the same pinned shapes
           (CAC_ONTOLOGY_VERSION; tests/graphs/test_term_resolution.py, pyshacl).
  ontologies: the SDK writes CASE/UCO 1.5.0 terms. CaseLinker validates against its own pinned
              copies (CAC_ONTOLOGY_VERSION), not against ontologies bundled in the SDK.
  known gaps: validation.graph.validate_graph_file resolves extensions from the SDK monorepo
              layout (extensions/, ontology/), so a pip install cannot validate legalproc or CAC
              terms with it; CaseLinker runs pyshacl over its vendored shapes instead
              (state_machines/graphs/shacl.py).
  PACER graphs: emitted by state_machines/graphs/emit.py with this release.
